@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Random Forests — Decorrelated Tree Averaging
' Paper: Random Forests
' URL:   https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1010933404324
' Level: 1

database  "Training set\nn rows" as DATA <<db>>
component "Bootstrap sample k\nn rows drawn with replacement" as BOOT <<async>>
component "Tree k\nsplit on a random feature subset" as TREE <<app>>
component "Aggregate\nvote or average" as AGG <<host>>
component "Out-of-bag estimate\nrows this tree never saw" as OOB <<db>>

DATA ARROW_QUEUE BOOT : repeat for k = 1..K
BOOT ARROW_MAIN TREE : grow deep, no pruning
TREE ARROW_MAIN AGG : one prediction each
BOOT ARROW_OPTIONAL OOB : ~37% of rows excluded
OOB ARROW_OPTIONAL AGG : free generalization estimate

note bottom of AGG
  **Two sources of randomness, not one.** Bootstrap
  rows alone leave trees correlated, and averaging
  correlated predictors barely reduces variance.
  Drawing a **random subset of features at every
  split** is what decorrelates them — the step that
  makes the ensemble work.
  ----
  Because each bootstrap omits roughly a third of
  the rows, the out-of-bag rows give a held-out
  error estimate without a separate validation
  split — useful when data is scarce.
end note
@enduml
